# Separate Sign-In and Sign-Up Pages Plan

## Problem Analysis

**Current Issue:**

- AuthActivity combines both sign-in and sign-up on one page
- Display Name field is visible even for sign-in users
- Confusing UX with both buttons visible simultaneously

**Desired Behavior:**

- Main authentication page (SignInActivity) shows only:
  - Email field
  - Password field
  - Sign In button
  - Google Sign-In button
  - Link to "Don't have an account? Sign Up"
- Separate Sign-Up page (SignUpActivity) shows:
  - Display Name field
  - Email field
  - Password field
  - Sign Up button
  - Link to "Already have an account? Sign In"

## Implementation Steps

### Step 1: Rename AuthActivity to SignInActivity

**Files to modify:**

- `app/src/main/java/com/cashpal/app/auth/AuthActivity.kt` → Rename to `SignInActivity.kt`
- `app/src/main/res/layout/activity_auth.xml` → Rename to `activity_sign_in.xml`
- `app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml` → Update activity declaration

**Changes in SignInActivity.kt:**

- Remove `etDisplayName` references
- Remove `btnSignUp` button and its click listener
- Keep only sign-in and Google sign-in functionality
- Add navigation to SignUpActivity when "Sign Up" link is clicked

**Changes in activity_sign_in.xml:**

- Remove Display Name field
- Remove "Sign Up" button
- Add "Don't have an account? Sign Up" clickable text at the bottom
- Keep Email, Password, Sign In button, and Google Sign-In button

### Step 2: Create SignUpActivity

**New files to create:**

- `app/src/main/java/com/cashpal/app/auth/SignUpActivity.kt`
- `app/src/main/res/layout/activity_sign_up.xml`

**SignUpActivity.kt implementation:**

- Include Display Name, Email, and Password fields
- Single "Sign Up" button
- Google Sign-In button (optional, as Google handles both sign-in and sign-up)
- Navigation back to SignInActivity with "Already have an account? Sign In" link
- Validate all fields before sign-up
- On successful sign-up, navigate to MainActivity

**activity_sign_up.xml design:**

- Display Name field (required for sign-up)
- Email field
- Password field
- Sign Up button (primary)
- Optional: Google Sign-In button
- "Already have an account? Sign In" clickable text
- Same visual style as sign-in page

### Step 3: Update AndroidManifest.xml

**Changes:**

- Rename `.auth.AuthActivity` to `.auth.SignInActivity`
- Add new `.auth.SignUpActivity` (not exported)
- Ensure SignInActivity remains the launcher activity

### Step 4: Update Navigation Flow

**SignInActivity:**

- When user clicks "Sign Up" link → Navigate to SignUpActivity
- After successful sign-in → Navigate to MainActivity
- After successful Google sign-in → Navigate to MainActivity

**SignUpActivity:**

- When user clicks "Sign In" link → Navigate back to SignInActivity (finish current)
- After successful sign-up → Navigate to MainActivity
- After successful Google sign-in → Navigate to MainActivity (Google handles both)

### Step 5: Improve UX/UI

**Enhancements:**

- Add proper loading states during authentication
- Show password strength indicator on Sign Up page
- Add "Forgot Password?" link on Sign In page (future enhancement)
- Ensure proper keyboard handling (Next → Next → Done)
- Add form validation with helpful error messages

## File Structure After Changes

```
app/src/main/java/com/cashpal/app/auth/
├── SignInActivity.kt (renamed from AuthActivity.kt)
└── SignUpActivity.kt (new)

app/src/main/res/layout/
├── activity_sign_in.xml (renamed from activity_auth.xml)
└── activity_sign_up.xml (new)
```

## Expected User Flow

1. **App Launch** → SignInActivity (launcher)
2. **User without account** → Clicks "Sign Up" → SignUpActivity
3. **User enters details** → Clicks "Sign Up" → Account created → MainActivity
4. **User with account** → Enters credentials → Clicks "Sign In" → MainActivity
5. **User wants Google** → Clicks "Continue with Google" → Google picker → MainActivity
6. **User on wrong page** → Clicks "Sign In/Sign Up" link → Switches pages

## Key Benefits

- Cleaner, more intuitive UI
- Separate concerns (sign-in vs sign-up)
- Standard authentication flow (matches most apps)
- Reduces cognitive load for users
- Easier to maintain and extend
- Better form validation per context

